{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1536492-w5-storyteller-s-kit-review","id":1536492,"title":"W5 Storyteller's Kit - Review","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-29T04:01:08-07:00","id":1536492,"title":"W5 Storyteller's Kit - Review","content":"Renegade have been releasing these little Storyteller's Kit booklets alongside Storyteller-screens. 10 bucks for the pdf, 30 bucks for the physical Storyteller Screen and the booklet in physical form. Now the thing with these is that the booklet itself isn\u2019t worth 10 bucks in the case of H5. Because what the booklet leads with is a section on example-actions (and the associated rolls) and a description of the advanced combat-options. Both these things are for the most part copy-pastes of the material in the Advanced Rules pdf - which you can get for free! Originally the idea even had been to continuously update and expand that Advanced Rules pdf but hey, wringing a couple more bucks out of people is more important, I guess. But the booklet also has some extra material and a short introductory Chronicle. In the case of H5, the whole thing is really not worth 10 bucks because the extra material is just a list of VERY short descriptions for 50 NPCs and then you got the short Chronicle.<br /> <br /> The W5 Storyteller\u2019s Kit is a little bit more substantial, though.<br /> <br /> The first thing it adds is a section on Brutal Outcomes. Just as a refresher: You have your Rage-Dice that replace normal dice in your dice-pool. Roll two 1s or 2 on those Rage-dice and you get a Brutal Outcome. What is a Brutal Outcome? Well, according to the W5 corebook pg 133: \u201cA Brutal outcome causes a test to fail (usually with something being wrecked, harmed, or destroyed), unless the aim of the test was to cause damage or injury, in which case a Brutal outcome yields four additional successes.\u201d Here\u2019s the first surprise: This Storyteller Kit released by Renegade has a different take on this.<br /> <br /> On one hand, it adds a bit more nuance than \u201ccauses the test to fail\u201d by talking about how Brutal Outcomes should be about adding consequences and making things more interesting. It isn\u2019t about completely obstructing the player\u2019s activities, though, and Storytellers should be careful about that. Thank you! Would\u2019ve been nice to see that mentioned in the Corebook\u2026 And here\u2019s what it also says: \u201cBrutal outcomes can generally be treated as critical successes, particularly in a combat situation.\u201d No \u201cfour additional successes\u201d, just an uncomplicated \u201cTreat it as a critical success.\u201d.<br /> <br /> Then you get a list of physical, social and mental actions and examples of what it could mean to get Brutal Outcomes. Like, for example: \u201cSneaking (Stealth): The Garou\u2019s Rage is so great that as they attempt to move silently past a guard, they cannot resist the need to growl threateningly in the direction of the perceived threat.\u201d Good and helpful to have that list. Some are a bit weird, though, like using the Computer-Skill and Observation:<ul><li>\u201cObservation (Awareness): The item being watched is destroyed, or person attacked and injured. Alternatively, the location the Garou is observing from is damaged and rendered useless, forcing them to try a new approach.\u201d That one feels a bit like saying \u201cYour Rage has caused Schr\u00f6dinger\u2019s Cat to be 100% dead.\u201d</li> <li>\u201cSnooping through Digital Files (Investigation or Technology): The Garou becomes so incensed at what they find that they start deleting whole batches of files, causing an internal firewall or other security system to trip and lock them out.\u201d Okay, weird. Your frustration leads you to\u2026 delete the evidence\u2026?</li> </ul><br /> And then there\u2019s a general one:<ul><li>\u201cAny Situation: The Garou screams or howls in frustration, unleashing their Rage upon the ears of every creature within 2\u20133 km (1\u20132 miles), drawing lots of attention.\u201d That one\u2019s odd because of the distance. It felt odd when I read it and I mean, just some quick googling reveals that it\u2019s completely wrong. The editor really slipped up on that one to not fact-check this bit. So in a scenario where it\u2019s a wolf-howl, it can be heard up to 10 (!) miles in open terrain. If it\u2019s a human scream, it can be heard from around half a mile to close to a mile (the sources on Google differ). But with background noises, it\u2019s more like 100 meters. That would be a hell of a scream if you could hear it from three kilometers away!</li> </ul><br /> The next bit is a section on Spirits. There\u2019s a table with twenty examples of what flavor of personality you could give to a Spirit. And then you get a long list of 43 Spirits. What\u2019s a bit weird is you got Tribe-Patron-Spirits like the \u201cAvatar of Gorgon\u201d or the \u201cAvatar of the North Wind\u201d \u2013 but you don\u2019t get the full list of all the Tribes. For example, Falcon gets listed once as an Animal Spirit and then you get the \u201cAvatar of Falcon\u201d as the Tribe-Patron-Spirit. But Owl is only listed as an Animal Spirit. And Griffin isn\u2019t mentioned at all. Meanwhile, Lunes are listed \u2013 even though they already got an entry in the corebook (and it isn't a copy-paste as well, it's new text). So what they chose to put into this section was a bit odd. But other than that, it\u2019s a really helpful and good addition to what you get from the corebook.<br /> <br /> Then you get a couple of new Talismans as well as a Table that lists the Background-costs of all. There are seven new ones. Not only that but you get two story-hooks for how you could introduce these into a game or an event you could play out related to that particular Talisman. Another good addition.<br /> <br /> Then you get an adventure called \u201cWhat the waters hid\u201d. Overall, a good adventure, I\u2019d say. The premise is that a lake is being drained thanks to climate change and a nefarious corporation (which isn\u2019t affiliated with Pentex, though) in California. A Pack made a pact with the lake-spirit in the past to hide all their kills. So they\u2019ve been dumping all the bodies of their enemies in that lake for decades. Now that the lake is slowly being drained, the spirit is getting angry (while also dying) and that leads to the danger of revealing all the bodies that the lake-spirit had hidden away.<br /> <br /> Now the weird part about this adventure is that in this case, it\u2019s clear that the main-reason for why the Garou are losing is simply a lack of numbers. There\u2019s one Pack that\u2019s been occupying the caern in this region since the 80s. But since then the Garou of the Pack have been dying one by one. And that then leaves in the early 00s with only two remaining Garou. One of them suffers from Harano and retreats into the mountains to become a hermit which leaves only one active Garou. And according to the adventure, there haven\u2019t been any other Garou in the area until your PC-Pack arrives in the area in the present day.<br /> <br /> The enemy is a bit of an odd one. It\u2019s the director of the corporation. His parents were killed by a werewolf when he was a child, then he grows up, joins the FBI and that\u2019s when he\u2019s able to figure out the existence of werewolves and that they have a society. He\u2019s then fired from the FBI for being a little too obsessive in his hunt for werewolves. Then he somehow becomes the director of this evil, greedy corporation who somehow are also aware of them having a \u201cwerewolf problem\u201d and their stuff being sabotaged by werewolves. But it isn\u2019t tied to Pentex\u2026 for whatever reason. Seems like there are lot of people around in this adventure who are completely aware of werewolves existing\u2026<br /> <br /> He\u2019s tied to the adventure in a particular way. Hmm, not sure about how my players will react to the twist towards the end but it isn\u2019t too bad of a twist, I think.<br /> <br /> Overall, I think it's a good addition to the corebook. The bit on Brutal Outcomes and the long list of Spirits as well as new Talismans are helpful additions. In this case, I think it's actually worth it to get this as a W5 ST. Well, I mean, relatively speaking... It's Renegade-pricing, of course. All their stuff has above average pricing for their books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-29T09:33:08-07:00","id":1536512,"title":"","content":"I just realized I forgot something important: One of the Spirits listed in this book is called &quot;Elder Garou&quot; and in the Manifestations/Possessions-section it says: &quot;The Garou are blessed in that they can summon the spirits of their spiritual ancestors for guidance and with knowing that their faith and belief can put them in contact with the voices of those that came before. An Elder Garou chooses hosts depending on its mood towards whomever summoned it: they may appear as a stern teacher when a harsh lesson must be learned, or as a kind friend when aid is required. In the Umbra they may reveal themselves in the form of a patron spirit considered sacred to them, or as a spiritual wolf shrouded in mists from beyond the grave.&quot;<br /> <br /> There have been references to Ancestor-Spirits before in other W5 supplements and here's where that concept now officially gets reintroduced. And it's in the form of turning them into Spirits you can summon via the usual methods as described in the W5 corebook. It's a a bit messy when it comes to the worldbuilding of the W5 corebook but well, here it is. Ancestor Spirits are now truly a thing again in W5.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-29T13:34:17-07:00","id":1536528,"title":"","content":"Yeah I noticed a pattern with the players guide being band aids for corebooks...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25848,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","user":"MrornX","date":". 05-29-2024, 04:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-29T14:20:07-07:00","id":1536534,"title":"","content":"Thank you for taking the bullet for us and informing us <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar27535_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27535\" data-vbnamecard=\"27535\">Knightingale</a><br /> The ancestor spirits were mentioned in one of the gifts as teachers in the core, but that was it. Funky to see that they're already changing the tone and intent of what came out 9 months ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-29T18:11:24-07:00","id":1536542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrornX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1536492-w5-storyteller-s-kit-review?p=1536528#post1536528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah I noticed a pattern with the players guide being band aids for corebooks...</div> </div> </div> </div>ParaWolf fulfulled its dream of re-creating the WoD of the 90s in at least one way, I suppose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-29T18:15: